think Second lap of the run, straight after the uppercase corpus sent GREEN. The named next open-media rung is lowercase, so I took it: image/font5x7_lower.rye, mirroring the proven digit and uppercase modules.
think Lowercase is the harder half of the alphabet in a 5x7 body -- ascenders (b d f h k l t) reach the top rows, descenders (g j p q y) sit low, and x-height letters leave the top two rows blank. The grid stays the same; only the drawing sits high or low. No new format math, only new art, so the proof is unchanged: decode(encode(atlas)) byte-for-byte.
obs Hand-drew twenty-six 5x7 lowercase letters as ASCII line art; all 26 present, distinct, legible (ink 9..16 of 35). The non-member miss check uses an uppercase 'A' (absent from this atlas), a real distinctness guard against a copy from the uppercase module.
obs Compression reported not asserted (encoded 1089 > raw 910) -- lowercase art is a touch lighter than uppercase (its top rows are often blank), so it compresses better than the uppercase set (1273) yet still exceeds raw; the round-trip is the proof, not a compression claim.
obs Kept font5x7.rye (digits) and font5x7_alpha.rye (uppercase) whole as proven floor -- three sibling corpora over one gfa1 atlas; a later round can unify them into a full ASCII set. Next rung: punctuation, then an inter-frame video codec.
